+The systematic use of AdvP premodifiers is the strongest internal-syntactic objection to the D-noun analysis. In the other three noun subcategories, internal premodifiers are normally adjectival or nominal. Adverbs occurring before a whole NP, as in \mention{only you}, don't supply a parallel: they're peripheral modifiers of the NP, rather than internal modifiers of its head \citep[430--431]{huddleston2002}. The distinction concerns attachment as well as word order.
+
+\textcite[42--47]{payne2010} establish that adverbs can postmodify common nouns, and they analyse \mention{almost} as modifying the attributive noun \mention{textbook} in \mention{an almost textbook case} \citep[p.~75, n.~3]{payne2010}. Adverbial modification therefore isn't categorically excluded from Noun. But neither postmodification nor a restricted attributive use removes the systematic contrast between \mention{hardly any} or \mention{almost every} and ordinary common-noun premodification.
+
+The existing noun subcategories also differ in the range and conditions of their postmodifiers. Common nouns freely take integrated relative clauses; personal pronouns permit a restricted range, including \mention{we who have read the report} \citep[430]{huddleston2002}. Compound determinatives permit them too, as in \mention{something that you need to know} \citep[423--424]{huddleston2002}. These comparisons show that modifier permissions can vary within Noun, but they don't neutralize the premodifier objection.
+
+Including determinatives requires stating the usual premodifier contrast below primary Noun. The determinative subcategory licenses \mention{hardly any} and \mention{almost every}; common nouns license \mention{experienced teacher}. Neither \ungram{\mention{experienced every}} nor \ungram{\mention{almost teacher}} follows. This preserves the judgments, but preservation alone doesn't favour the reclassification. Shared nominal structure elsewhere has to justify locating this contrast at subcategory level. Section~\ref{sec:costs} weighs that cost against the projection rules in §\ref{sec:fragment}.
+
+\subsection{Word formation and category boundaries}\label{sec:word-formation}
+
+Determinatives participate in word formation both as components of compounds and as potential bases for derivatives in other categories. \textit{CGEL} explicitly cites \mention{nothingness} and \mention{oneness} among formations with \mention{-ness} on non-adjectival bases \citep[Ch.~19, §5.7.2(i)]{huddleston2002}. Further established nouns include \mention{thisness}, \mention{fewness} and \mention{muchness}; the adverb \mention{mostly} is related to \mention{most} by \mention{-ly}.\footnote{See \textit{Merriam-Webster.com Dictionary}, s.vv.\ \href{https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/thisness}{\mention{thisness}} and \href{https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/fewness}{\mention{fewness}}, and \textit{Dictionary.com}, s.vv.\ \href{https://www.dictionary.com/browse/muchness}{\mention{muchness}} and \href{https://www.dictionary.com/browse/mostly}{\mention{mostly}} (accessed 10 September 2026). \mention{Thisness} is a philosophical term; \mention{muchness} has restricted senses and uses. The entries establish the words and document their meanings or histories; they don't establish a uniformly productive modern rule.} These relationships extend beyond the formation of determinatives themselves.
+
+The suffixes suggest an adjectival affinity worth testing. The default suffix for forming nouns from adjectives is \mention{-ness}, and adverbial \mention{-ly} likewise primarily attaches to adjectives. Neither is exclusively adjective-selecting: \textit{CGEL} gives the non-adjectival \mention{-ness} bases just mentioned and noun-based adverbs such as \mention{partly} and \mention{purposely} \citep[566; Ch.~19, §5.7.2(i)]{huddleston2002}. Thus the derivatives are compatible with several base classifications. Their evidential force depends on the suffix's input range and the relevant sense of the base.
+
+This comparison distinguishes derivation from the category of its output. On a direct D-base analysis, \mention{nothingness} maps determinative to common noun; the D-noun proposal places that change within Noun. \mention{Mostly} maps its base to adverb, crossing a primary-category boundary under either taxonomy. Where an independently motivated converted base intervenes, the derivation begins from that base instead. A converted noun shouldn't be posited solely to protect a preferred classification of D.
+
+Compounding supplies another comparison. Common and proper nouns readily form compounds and derivatives such as \mention{friendship} and \mention{Londoner}; pronouns enter the gender-marking compounds \mention{he-goat} and \mention{she-ass} \citep[Ch.~19, §§5.3, 5.6.2(b), 5.7.2(k)]{huddleston2002}. Determinative bases combine with nominal material in \mention{someone} and \mention{anything}, and their premodifier permissions recur in the compounds (§\ref{sec:compounds}). A closed lexical inventory can therefore participate in word formation, though the processes and their productivity differ among the subclasses.
+
+Numeral morphology extends the comparison to further outputs. Cardinal bases enter ordinal and fractional formations, analysed respectively as adjectival and nominal by \textcite{reynolds2026numerals}. Their formation also supplies evidence about the boundary between a numeral lexeme and a syntactic phrase. That constituency question is separate from whether a cardinal base belongs within Noun. The broader inventory includes both numeral and nonnumeral derivations, whose input restrictions need to be represented under either classification.
+
+Morphology contributes a mixed profile. Number and genitive connect determinatives with existing nouns, while grade and the predominantly adjectival \mention{-ness} and \mention{-ly} patterns connect some members with adjectives. These processes cut across the proposed subcategories in different ways. The D-noun analysis preserves their separate input restrictions; the positive case for its superordinate grouping rests chiefly on the recurring nominal constructions examined next.
 
